2026-09-01 (later)  Ambient-probe consistency check, and the paper finalised
================================================================================
New capability:
  - observability.check_ambient_consistency. At quasi-steady the model fixes the
    top-oil rise above ambient, so the record implies an ambient temperature;
    differencing that against the measured channel isolates the ambient probe,
    which every other calculation here trusted without ever testing it.
    A persistent offset is a siting bias. A component that changes with cooling
    stage is cooler airflow reaching the probe, and nothing else available
    separates it, because a model error does not know which fans are running.
  - The direction matters: a probe reading WARM biases the identified rated oil
    rise LOW and the loading envelope HIGH, which is the unsafe direction. The
    check names it.
  - Implemented as a flag, never a correction. The offset absorbs every
    steady-state model error as well as the probe. It declines to report when
    the load never settles for three oil time constants.
  - Method due to L. Paulhiac of EDF, given in correspondence, and credited in
    the manuscript acknowledgement. THAT WORDING IS NOT YET APPROVED BY HIM.
Paper:
  - Section VI gains the screening paragraph for the above, attached to the
    corruption battery's own result that omitted ambient is the largest single
    defect and acts in the unsafe direction.
  - Section V now records the viscosity falsification as two-directional support
    for keeping ON and OD separate.
  - Section I engages the 2022 article instead of talking around it.
  - Acknowledgement extended to ambient-measurement validation.
  - Dates moved to September 2026. Rebuilt: 5 pages plus a 2-page supplement,
    no undefined references, no overfull boxes.
Defect found and fixed:
