2026-09-03  Rated current confirmed; the gradient offset is placement, not calibration
============================================================================
The DynaLoad dataset author confirmed the HV rated current is 175 A. The 132 kV
base already in use derives 174.95 A, a 0.03 % difference, so the 0.048-0.429 pu
load hull is confirmed rather than assumed. That upgrades the central negative
finding -- the replication gap does not close on this record -- from an
engineering estimate to a fact resting on the owner's own rating. It also
disarms a trap: the voltage channel reads the LV side while the current is the
HV winding, and an inference in private/sintef/characterise.py that the measured
voltage identifies the winding was wrong. Corrected there.

He also pushed back on our description of the winding-below-oil behaviour,
reading "the two channels do not share a reference point" as a claim of
calibration error, and stating he has no reason to believe an offset exists.
He was right, and the wording invited it. What was meant was sensor HEIGHT in
the oil column. His own data settles it: across 6,247 quasi-steady samples the
deficit is not constant but shrinks monotonically with load and crosses zero
inside the measured hull, from -7.06 K at 0.05-0.10 pu to +2.27 K at
0.30-0.43 pu on Hot-spot HV. A fixed calibration offset cannot do that. Fitted C
is 8.93-10.41 K across all four winding channels against a measured mean
top-minus-bottom oil span of 10.37 K, and channels sitting lower in the winding
show a larger C.

New result, and the reason for the code change. On a light-load record the
rated gradient is not identified but the CROSSING LOAD is. Sweeping the exponent
from y = 1.0 to 2.0 moves the fitted rated gradient from 40.1 to 99.1 K, a
factor of 2.5, while K* moves from 0.261 to 0.268 pu -- 2.7 %. The residual
barely changes, so the record cannot even choose y. The identifiable quantity is
the ratio C/Delta_hr = K*^y, not either parameter alone. Confirmed independently
by the production fit, which runs a free exponent, lands at y = 0.63-0.98, and
still returns K* = 0.261.

Code:
  - observability.GradientDatumCheck gains crossing_load_pu, and the note says
    whether the crossing is inside the record's hull (measured) or above it
    (extrapolated), and to prefer it to the rated gradient.
  - The docstring now states the mechanism is height in the oil column and
    explicitly NOT a calibration error, with the public record cited.
Suite 259 -> 262. Three new tests: the crossing is recovered where the channels
cross, it survives a wrong exponent where the rated gradient does not, and a
crossing above the hull is labelled an extrapolation.

Open and unexplained, recorded rather than smoothed over: if the hot-spot probes
sit at the designed hot spot and Top oil is winding-outlet oil, C for those
channels should be 1-2 K, not the 8.93-9.21 K fitted. Three candidates and no
basis to choose -- the top-oil reference is hotter than the probe's local oil;
the probes are lower than "hot spot" implies; or Delta_hr*K^y does not hold at
0.05-0.43 pu in ONAN. One question asked; no assumption made.
